//! check: fail(E1001)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.region.edge.elem, mem.tier0.move.2
//!
//! s167 (wolf-lang#385, ruled option 3) — the fast path, spelled.
//! A plain `push` COPIES its element (`push_copies_element.lu`), so
//! the caller keeps its value; `push(take x)` hands the element over
//! for good and `x` is dead after the call.
//!
//! This is the half of the ruling that keeps the cheap path available
//! without making it the silent default: option 2 (a silent take)
//! would have made `push` the one place in the language where handing
//! a value over plainly destroys it, which hands a newcomer a
//! use-after-move exactly where plain code is supposed to be safe.
//! Spelled, it is an ordinary move and the ordinary diagnostic —
//! E1001 with the move site named, and `copy x` the fix the ladder
//! already prescribes.
//!
//! `take` is admitted here at all because a store is the one `read`
//! position that KEEPS what it was lent; every other `read` parameter
//! refuses a call-site mode outright (X1, E1007).

fn main() -> !int {
    var xs = List[int]()
    (mut xs).push(1)
    var outs = List[List[int]]()
    (mut outs).push(take xs)
    (mut xs).push(4)                 // ERROR E1001: moved into `outs`
    print("outs0={outs[0].len} xs={xs.len}")
    0
}
